--- AGENT RESPONSE (FULL) ---
Length: 9053 chars
Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Good — below is a focused, practical checklist and set of questions you can bring to your meeting with Robert Kim. It’s organized so you can quickly cover the key decision points (should you accept a settlement or push to trial), plus the documents and preparations you should have ready.

Use the sections as an agenda: start with the Big Picture questions, then go through Evidence & Damages, Costs & Timeline, Settlement Terms & Strategy, Trial Readiness, and finish with Administrative items and next steps.

Big-picture questions to ask Robert
- What is your realistic assessment of our strengths and weaknesses if this goes to hearing/trial? (Ask for percentages or ranges if he can estimate likelihood of winning and expected recovery.)
- What are the likely outcomes the court could enter (full damages, partial, nothing, counterclaims)? What’s the likely range of damages?
- Do we have any strong legal defenses or procedural risks I should know about?
- What would you recommend given my objectives (money only, reputation, injunctive relief, confidentiality, future business relationships)?
- What is your recommended settlement threshold or “walk-away” bottom line? How did you arrive at that number?
- What are the realistic chances of prevailing on appeal and would an appeal be worth the cost/effort?

Evidence, documentation, and what the court will want
- Confirm the critical documents the court will expect and that you should prepare:
  - Executed contract(s) and any amendments
  - All communications with the opposing party (emails, texts, DMs, letters), organized chronologically
  - Invoices, payment records, bank statements, ledger entries, payroll records
  - Work product, deliverables, project timelines, meeting notes
  - Personnel records or HR documents if employment elements are relevant
  - Discovery responses, deposition transcripts, interrogatories, and produced exhibits
  - Any expert reports or appraisals (or estimates of whether one might be needed)
- Ask whether anything needs formal authentication or a witness to lay foundation (e.g., printouts of electronic communications).
- Ask whether any evidence is weak or needs bolstering (additional witness statements, forensic accounting, metadata preservation).
- Chain-of-custody or preservation: Are there any digital preservation steps I should still take (backup devices, preserve email accounts, avoid deleting messages)?

Damages, valuation, and proof
- How are damages calculated in our claim (contract damages, consequential, lost profits, liquidated damages)?
- Are there offsetting damages or counterclaims the opposing party will assert?
- Are attorney fees recoverable under the contract or statute? If so, how are those handled (motion for fees, cap)?
- Ask for guidance on quantifying damages: what documentation will the court want to see to support each element (ledgers, profit/loss schedules, expert testimony)?
- If there’s uncertainty in damages, would a damages expert strengthen the case and what is the estimated cost?

Costs, timeline, and risk
- What is the estimated cost to take this to hearing/trial (attorney fees, expert fees, filing fees, travel, depositions)? Provide a low/medium/high estimate.
- How long until a hearing/trial can realistically be scheduled in San Diego County housing court? What are next procedural dates (pretrial conference, exchange of exhibits, trial briefs)?
- How long is the expected hearing/trial and what is the courtroom format (bench trial vs jury)? If jury is an option, what are the pros/cons here?
- What are the financial and non-financial risks of going to trial (time, stress, publicity, possibility of losing and paying opposing fees)?
- If we settle now versus after pre-trial steps (e.g., after a deposition or expert report), how might the case value change?

Settlement terms and negotiation strategy
- What is our target settlement range and your recommended opening/anchor positions?
- What non-monetary terms should we consider (confidentiality, non-disparagement, mutual release, non-compete, return of property, payment schedule)?
- If we accept a settlement, how will the release be drafted? Will it be mutual or one-sided? Ask to review sample/standard release language.
- If payment is to be made over time, what security or enforcement mechanisms should we require (promissory note, lien, escrow, personal guaranty)?
- Are there tax consequences to a settlement we should consider? (Recommend consulting a tax advisor if needed.)
- Is mediation or neutral evaluation recommended now? What are the advantages of trying mediation before a hearing?
- What are the confidentiality and public-record implications of settlement vs a court judgment?

Trial readiness and tactical questions
- If we decide to go to trial, what additional discovery or preparation would materially improve our position (e.g., further depositions, expert reports, subpoenas for documents)?
- Which witnesses are essential? Do we have their availability and written statements? Are any witnesses hostile or unavailable?
- Do we need any expert witnesses? If so, when would we need to retain them and what is the likely cost?
- What pretrial motions do you anticipate (motions in limine, summary adjudication, motion to exclude experts)?
- What exhibits will be used and have they been pre-marked and exchanged per local rules?
- What is our tactic for cross-examination of the opposing party and rebuttal evidence?
- How will you present damages to the judge (or jury); do we need demonstrative exhibits or charts?

Enforcement, collection, and practical recovery issues
- If we obtain a judgment, how likely is collection? Does the former partner have assets or is there risk they can’t pay?
- Should we investigate the opposing party’s assets now (bank accounts, business ownership, real property) to assess collectability?
- If the opposing party owes attorney’s fees, how are those awarded and collected?
- Are there post-judgment remedies we should plan for (levy, writ of execution, garnishment, liens)?

Administrative and ethical considerations
- Are there any conflicts of interest or ethical matters I should know about?
- What level of settlement authority do you need from me (e.g., can you accept X or do you need to call me)?
- How will we communicate during settlement talks and trial prep (email vs phone)? How quickly do you need responses?
- What deadlines should I calendar now (hearing date, settlement offers expiration, document exchange)?

Document checklist to bring/organize before your meeting
- Master copy of the contract(s) and any amendments
- Chronological folder of communications (emails, texts) with dates and short labels
- Financial documents supporting damages (invoices, bank statements, ledgers)
- Copy of pleadings, discovery responses, deposition transcripts, exhibits produced
- List of potential witnesses with contact info and short bullet of what each will say
- Any expert or consultant reports, or names of prospective experts
- Your personal notes/timeline of events (one-page summary for attorney)

Practical preparation: create these quick tools for your attorney
- One-page case summary (facts, timeline, key evidence, desired outcomes)
- Damages worksheet (line items for claimed damages, with supporting docs)
- “Must-have” vs “Nice-to-have” settlement terms list (monetary and non-monetary)
- BATNA (best alternative to negotiated agreement): what you will do if you don’t settle

Sample email/script to Robert Kim (short)
Hi Robert — I’d like to meet to evaluate whether to accept a settlement or proceed to hearing/trial. Please let me know your available times this week. Agenda items I want to cover:
1) your assessment of likely outcomes and recommended settlement range;
2) estimated costs to go to trial and timeline in San Diego County housing court;
3) what additional discovery or evidence would materially increase our recovery;
4) proposed settlement terms and enforcement/security options;
5) next steps if we proceed to hearing.
I’ll bring a one-page case summary, all discovery, and my damages worksheet. — [Your name]

Other tips and reminders
- Authorization limit: decide and communicate in advance how much settlement authority you’ll give your lawyer (e.g., up to $X without phoning you).
- Confidentiality: insist on a confidentiality clause if you care about secrecy; discuss whether a mutual release is appropriate.
- Timing: sometimes settlement value changes after key events (depositions, expert reports). Ask whether it’s worth waiting for specific milestones before negotiating.
- Preservation: since discovery just ended, preserve everything — don’t delete communications and keep originals safe.

If you’d like, I can:
- Draft a one-page case summary template for you to fill in before meeting Robert.
- Create a damages worksheet template.
- Convert the sample email into a ready-to-send email addressed to Robert (if you give me his email).

Which of those (summary, worksheet, email) would you like me to prepare now?

