Good — I can help you get organized. Below I’ve put together (A) a set of focused questions to take to Robert Kim, grouped by topic; (B) a practical evidence/documents checklist so you can be sure you have what the court and your lawyer will expect; (C) a simple decision framework for “settle vs. trial” (including a quick expected‑value approach you can use with your attorney); and (D) next procedural steps and trial‑prep items to ask about. At the end I’ll ask a few quick clarifying questions and offer to draft a short email or meeting script for you.

A. Key questions to ask your attorney (organized)
1) Case strength and outcome likelihood
- Based on discovery, how strong is our case on the key elements of the contract claim? What are the strongest and weakest pieces of evidence?
- What is the realistic range of recoverable damages (low / likely / high)?
- What is your best professional assessment of our probability of winning at trial?

2) Settlement valuation and strategy
- What settlement range would you recommend considering the evidence, costs, and timeline?
- What is the minimum acceptable settlement you recommend (net to me after fees/costs/taxes)?
- Are there non‑monetary terms I should consider (confidentiality, non‑disparagement, non‑compete, future business restrictions, release language)?
- Should we ask for security/escrow or structured payments if the defendant can’t pay a lump sum?

3) Costs, fees, and financial considerations
- How much more will pursuing trial likely cost (legal fees, expert fees, court costs, etc.)?
- How will fees be billed going forward? How do you estimate net recovery after fees?
- Are there fee‑shifting provisions in the contract or statute (i.e., can we recover attorney’s fees if we win)?

4) Risks, defenses and counterclaims
- What counterclaims or offsets might the opposing party pursue or has pled?
- What are the key risks (evidence gaps, witness credibility problems, adverse rulings, sanctions, collection/enforcement problems post‑judgment)?

5) Procedural and timing questions
- What are the next court deadlines and typical timeline for getting a hearing/trial date in San Diego housing court?
- Are there required pretrial filings (pretrial conference statements, exhibit lists, witness lists, motions in limine)? Deadlines?
- Is this a bench trial or jury trial? Do we need to demand a jury (and by when) if we want one?

6) Alternatives to trial
- Would you recommend mediation or private settlement conferences before trial? If so, when and with which mediator?
- If we mediate, what authority will you request from me to accept an offer (i.e., do you need my prior approval above/below certain amounts)?

7) Enforcement/collection
- If we get a judgment, how likely is it we can collect? What steps would be needed to enforce/collect in San Diego County?
- Are there bankruptcy risks if the opposing party is insolvent?

8) Practical negotiation items (terms to include)
- If we settle, how do we draft the release? What exclusions (future claims, specific claims) should be included/excluded?
- Should settlement include confidentiality, non‑disparagement, and a mutual release?
- Should we include language on tax treatment, payment schedule, interest if late, and remedies for breach of settlement?

B. Evidence & document checklist (bring these to your attorney meeting)
- Executed contract(s), amendments, addenda, and signature pages
- All related communications: emails, text messages, chat logs, letters (sorted by date)
- Invoices, payment records, receipts, bank statements, payroll records, canceled checks
- Company documents: formation papers, ownership/partnership agreements, minutes/notes
- Records of performance or deliverables (work product, deliverable logs, acceptance notices)
- Any admissions by the opposing party (emails, recorded conversations, notes)
