- Exhibit binders and trial notebook: who prepares and how many copies?
- Motions: are any dispositive motions, summary judgment motions, or motions in limine recommended?
- Jury instructions / proposed findings (if applicable): who will draft?
- Schedule a settlement posture meeting where you establish bottom line/authorization levels and whether you give the lawyer authority to accept certain offers.
- Verify preservation of all evidence and steps to prevent spoliation.

Practical wording for settlement questions during the meeting
- “Given discovery, what settlement number would you recommend? What would my net recovery be after fees?”
- “If we go to trial, what is your estimated timeline and what additional costs should I expect?”
- “If we settle, what protections should be included in the release to protect me from future claims?”

Quick additional suggestions
- Prepare a one‑page timeline and an annotated folder of the top 10 documents (the ones that most strongly support your claim).
- Think now about your “walk‑away” number (minimum acceptable settlement after fees) and your “ideal” number so you can authorize the attorney quickly if an offer comes.
- Ask whether mediation should be scheduled proactively while waiting for a hearing date.

A brief disclaimer
- This is organizational and planning guidance — your attorney (Robert Kim) is the right person to give legal advice about your case specifics and to calculate risk and likely outcomes.

