Good — this is exactly the right time to get organized. Below I’ve put together (A) a checklist of questions to ask your attorney about settlement vs. trial, (B) the documents and evidence you should have ready, (C) key legal & practical considerations to weigh, and (D) a trial-readiness checklist and negotiation tips. If you want, I can draft an email or short agenda you can send to Robert Kim with these questions.

A. Questions to ask your attorney (use these in your meeting or call)
- Case assessment
  - Based on what you know now, what are the strengths and weaknesses of our case?
  - What is your best estimate of our likelihood of prevailing at hearing/trial (ranges are fine)?
  - What are the likely legal defenses the opposing party will raise, and how strong are they?
- Damages and remedies
  - What specific damages or remedies can we realistically recover (contract damages, lost profits, restitution, attorney fees, interest)?
  - How would you calculate a reasonable damages range for settlement (low/likely/high)?
  - Do we have a contract provision for attorney fees or costs that could shift fees?
- Costs, timing, and risk
  - What are the estimated additional attorney fees and costs to take this to hearing or trial (including experts, subpoenas, travel)?
  - How long until the hearing/trial if we proceed? What are the key upcoming deadlines?
  - What are the biggest risks of going to trial (adverse rulings, unexpected evidence, judge/jury reaction)?
- Practical enforceability
  - If we win, what are the realistic chances of collecting/ enforcing a money judgment against the opposing party (assets, insurance, wage garnishment)?
  - Does the opposing party have insurance that might cover some or all of a judgment?
- Settlement mechanics and strategy
  - What would you consider a reasonable settlement range given strengths/weaknesses and collection prospects?
  - Do you recommend mediation or settlement conference before trial? If so, when and who should pay for it?
  - What non-monetary terms would be important (release language, confidentiality, non-disparagement, return of property, future business restrictions)?
  - Should we insist on structured payments, security, or a promissory note if they cannot pay upfront?
- Evidence and admissibility
  - Are there any major evidentiary hurdles (hearsay, authentication, excluded documents)?
  - Do we need experts (e.g., damages expert, forensic accountant)? If so, what will that cost and how much will it help?
- Pretrial motions and appeals
  - Are there motions in limine, summary judgment, or other pretrial motions we should consider?
  - If we lose, what are the appeal prospects and the standard of review?
- Your role and expectations
  - What do you need from me now to be ready (documents, witnesses, witness prep)?
  - How will you communicate settlement offers to me and how much negotiation authority do you want from me (open offer, walk-away number)?
- San Diego County / court-specific questions
  - Are there local rules or judge-specific practices in San Diego housing court we should know about (time limits, evidence procedures, remote hearings)?
  - Is a jury trial available/appropriate in this court for this type of contract dispute?

B. Documents and evidence to gather & organize now
- The contract(s) at issue (signed versions, amendments, addenda)
- All communications: emails, text messages, instant messages, letters between you and the former partner/employer
- Payment records: invoices, receipts, bank statements, cancelled checks
- Work product or deliverables that show performance or breach
- Employment records, payroll, tax forms (if relevant)
- Financial records showing damages or lost profits (accounting reports, P&L, ledgers)
- Any written demands, settlement offers, or responses already exchanged
- Discovery materials you received: deposition transcripts, interrogatory answers, production documents
- Witness list with contact info and short summary of what each can testify to
- Expert reports (if already obtained) or notes on who might be an expert
- Timeline/chronology of key events (date-stamped, concise)
- Insurance policies (if either party has relevant coverage)
- Any prior judgments, liens, or bankruptcy filings by the opposing party (for collectability)

C. Key practical/legal considerations to weigh when deciding settlement vs trial
- Expected net recovery: estimate judgment amount minus expected trial costs and post-judgment collection costs
- Certainty vs risk: settlement gives certainty (even if less money) vs chance of higher award but risk of losing
- Time and stress: trials take time and emotional energy — factor this into your decision
- Enforceability: a big judgment is worthless if opponent has no assets or is judgment-proof
- Precedent/relationship: do you care about precedent or future relationship with the partner/employer?
- Confidentiality and reputation: settlement can include confidentiality and non-disparagement clauses
- Tax consequences: some recoveries are taxable — ask tax counsel or your attorney about tax treatment
- Non-monetary goals: sometimes you want injunctive relief, return of property, or a public vindication
- Cost of additional discovery or expert work required to tilt the outcome

D. Trial-readiness checklist (if you decide to push for trial)
- Finalized exhibit list and labeled exhibits (physical & electronic copies)
- Trial notebook with chronology, witness summaries, exhibits, legal authority
- Witness preparation and written declarations if helpful
- Motions in limine drafted and served (to exclude bad evidence)
- Trial brief and proposed findings or jury instructions (if applicable)
- Subpoenas ready for non-party witnesses or records
- Demonstrative exhibits/PowerPoints prepared for the hearing
- Plan for exhibit admission and authentication
- Budget for witness costs, expert fees, travel
- Post-judgment enforcement plan (what steps you will take if you win)

E. Negotiation and settlement tips to discuss with your attorney
- Determine your BATNA (best alternative to a negotiated agreement) and your reservation/walk-away value
- Consider structured settlement with security (e.g., escrow, promissory note with default remedies)
- Ask for a full release that covers all related claims and precise scope of the release
- Consider confidentiality/non-disparagement terms if reputation or future business is a concern
- Avoid admitting liability in any settlement language unless intentional
- If confidentiality is required, consider carve-outs for tax or future litigation obligations
- Use objective anchors (damages worksheets, expert opinions) when negotiating numbers
- Don’t reveal your bottom line; ask your attorney to negotiate offers and report back with options

