Assistant: Good — this is exactly the kind of preparation that will help you make a clear, informed decision. Below I’ve organized practical things to review and concrete questions you can ask Robert Kim. You can use the “questions to ask” list directly in a meeting or email, and I can also draft that email for you if you want.

1) Big-picture decision factors (what to be thinking about)
- Likelihood of winning at hearing/trial (as your lawyer sees it).
- Likely range of monetary recovery if you win (including interest) and what you’ll actually collect.
- Litigation costs (additional attorney fees, expert fees, court costs) and how long it will take.
- Non-monetary objectives (public record, precedent, reputational harms, confidentiality).
- Risk tolerance: chance you lose, partial win, or get an award lower than a settlement.
- Practical enforceability of any judgment (ability to collect from former partner).
- Tax consequences of any settlement or judgment.
- Emotional/time cost of continuing litigation versus settling.

2) Documents and evidence the court will want (checklist)
- The contract(s) at issue — signed pages and any amendments.
- All written communications relevant to the dispute (emails, texts, letters, instant messages).
- Financial records showing damages: invoices, bank statements, accounting records, ledgers, cancelled checks.
- Proof of performance or non-performance (work logs, deliveries, receipts).
- Witness statements or contact info for witnesses (employees, clients, third parties).
- Any contemporaneous notes or diaries that support your position.
- Discovery responses and produced documents (to show what’s already on the record).
- Expert reports (if any) and any appraisal/valuation evidence.
- Evidence of attempts to mitigate damages (proof you tried to reduce losses).
- Chain of title/ownership documents or business registration documents (if relevant).
- Any prior settlement offers or communications about settlement.

3) Questions to ask Robert Kim — core strategic questions
- Given the discovery record and our strongest evidence, what is our realistic range of recovery at hearing/trial?
- What’s your view of the probability of a full win, a partial win, or losing?
- What additional costs (attorney fees, expert fees, court fees) should I expect if we continue to hearing/trial?
- How long would it likely take to get a hearing/trial date in San Diego housing court, and how long might the hearing last?
- What are the risks at hearing/trial (legal, factual, evidentiary) you see now?
- Do we have any outstanding discovery issues or dispositive motions that could change the case value or leverage?
- How enforceable is a judgment against the opposing party? Do we have reason to believe they can pay or have assets?
- Would you recommend mediation or settlement conference before hearing? If so, when and under what terms?
- What are the most important settlement terms we should negotiate (beyond money) — for example confidentiality, non-disparagement, payment schedule, security for payments, mutual release?
- If the other side offers X (name a number), is that within an acceptable range? If not, what counter-offer strategy would you use?
- Are there any implications for attorney fees — can we recover fees from the other side if we win? If we settle, who pays our fees?
- What tax consequences should I be mindful of for a settlement or judgment?
- If we go to hearing and win, what is the likely timeline and cost to actually collect?
- If we settle, how binding is the release and what language do we need to protect our ongoing interests?
- When would you recommend accepting a settlement and when would you recommend going to trial?

4) Settlement-specific items to negotiate/confirm
- Total amount and payment structure (lump-sum vs installments).
- Timing of payments and late-payment penalties.
- Security for payment (promissory note, deed of trust, personal guarantee, escrow).
- Mutual or one-sided release: scope and whether it covers known & unknown claims.
- Confidentiality clause or public statement language.
- Non-disparagement clause.
- Allocation for attorney fees and costs (who pays these).
- Whether settlement is contingent on dismissal with prejudice and court sign-off.
- Whether there will be a covenant not to sue or future cooperation obligations.
- Tax allocation language (how payments are characterized).
- Stipulation about who pays costs for preparing/recording any security interest.
- Whether the settlement can be used as an admission in any other proceedings (usually no).

5) How to analyze settlement offers (simple expected-value framework)
- Estimate probable award if you go to hearing/trial (A).
- Estimate probability of winning that award (P).
- Estimate additional litigation costs to get to hearing/trial (C).
- Estimate chance and cost/effort to collect if you win (collection factor S between 0–1).
- Expected value if you go to trial ≈ P × (A × S) − C.
- Compare to settlement offer (immediate money, maybe less than A but lower risk), factoring in non-monetary terms.
- Example template: If A = $50,000, P = 60% (0.6), S = 0.8 (likely to collect 80%), C = $15,000 → EV ≈ 0.6 × (50k × 0.8) − 15k = 0.6 × 40k − 15k = 24k − 15k = 9k. If settlement offer is $20k now, settlement looks better.

