- Chronology/timeline of events (concise one-page summary)
- Witness list with contact info and short summary of what each will testify to
- Documents showing mitigation efforts (what you did to reduce damages)
- Any prior settlement offers/correspondence and trial preparation costs to date

D. Quick method to evaluate a settlement offer (expected value approach)
- Estimate a realistic probability of winning at trial (Pwin) — get attorney’s input.
- Estimate expected recoverable award if you win (Award).
- Estimate your total additional costs to trial (Costs) — attorney fees, expert fees, time.
- Estimate the chance, if you win, that you will actually collect the full award (CollectRate).
- Expected value (EV) of going to trial = Pwin * Award * CollectRate − (1 − Pwin) * Costs − Costs (or simply Pwin*(Award*CollectRate) − Costs).
- Compare EV to the settlement amount offered (net of tax/fees). Also factor non-monetary terms and risk tolerance (stress, time).

Example (simple):
- Pwin = 60% (0.6), Award = $100,000, CollectRate = 80% (0.8), Costs = $30,000
- EV = 0.6 * 100,000 * 0.8 − 30,000 = 48,000 − 30,000 = $18,000
